<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Noreia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Noreia was the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norici">Norici</a> in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, mentioned by Roman writers but never firmly located. Near it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> defeated a Roman army under Gnaeus Papirius Carbo in 113 BCE, and Caesar records that the Boii besieged it before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a>. It is generally sought in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/noreia.jpg" alt="Noreia" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atlas of Ancient and Classical Geography, 1907 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noricum_1907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Noreia's importance came from the iron of Noricum. The ore mined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, in the south of the region, was rich in manganese and almost free of phosphorus, and around 500 BCE the Celtic peoples of Noricum, chiefly the Taurisci, found that it made superior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>. A major production and trading centre grew up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a>, where specialised blacksmiths made weapons that were mostly exported south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, a Roman colony founded in 180 BCE. The steel known as chalybs Noricus supplied Roman swords from the mid-Republic onwards.</p><p>From about 200 BCE the tribes of Noricum appear to have been gradually united in a Celtic kingdom, the regnum Noricum, with Noreia as its capital according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. It became a key ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, trading weapons and tools for military protection without a formal treaty. When invaders entered Noricum in 113 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norici">Norici</a> made a desperate appeal and the Roman consul Gnaeus Papirius Carbo led an army over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, but he was heavily defeated near Noreia. Strabo and Appian both report this battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and Teutoni. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> in the 1st century CE, Noreia was already spoken of as a lost city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>It is not known whether the battlefield and the capital were the same place. On the basis of ancient distances, 18th-century writers placed Noreia near Murau or Neumarkt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, an identification that has repeatedly been questioned. The recorded distance of 1,200 stadia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> points rather to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, and scholars have proposed the settlements on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a> or the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollfeld">Zollfeld</a> plain, a sanctuary of the mother goddess Noreia near Liebenfels in the Glan valley, the Gurina settlement near Dellach, and the mining area of Hüttenberg.</p><p>The Tabula Peutingeriana, a 12th-century copy of a late Roman road map, seems to show both an older and a new Noreia, which may mean the name simply meant a Noric city, though a copyist's error is thought more probable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site has still not been verified. The view favoured today places it on the Gracarca mountain beside Lake Klopein in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, where a prehistoric hilltop settlement and several graves of Celtic princes have been found. After excavations at Sankt Margarethen near Mühlen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, the villagers renamed their village Noreia in 1930, although the finds there proved to be medieval.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Noreia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caesar records that thirty-two thousand Boii joined the Helvetii in 58 BCE after besieging <b>Noreia</b> in Noricum.</blockquote><a href="/state/boii-of-gaul">Boii of Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>Its core lay in modern Carinthia and Styria, around the Magdalensberg and a centre the Romans called <b>Noreia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Kingdom of Noricum</a></li></ul></section>
